Chien2022.pdf.md:7:## Neurodevelopmental model of schizophrenia revisited: similarity in individual deviation and idiosyncrasy from the normative model of whole-brain white matter tracts and shared brain-cognition covariation with ADHD and ASD
Chien2022.pdf.md:13:The neurodevelopmental model of schizophrenia is supported by multi-level impairments shared among schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders. Despite schizophrenia and typical neurodevelopmental disorders, i.e., autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and attention-de /uniFB01 cit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), as disorders of brain dysconnectivity, no study has ever elucidated whether whole-brain white matter (WM) tracts integrity alterations overlap or diverge between these three disorders. Moreover, whether the linked dimensions of cognition and brain metrics per the Research Domain Criteria framework cut across diagnostic boundaries remains unknown. We aimed to map deviations from normative ranges of whole-brain major WM tracts for individual patients to investigate the similarity and differences among schizophrenia (281 patients subgrouped into the /uniFB01 rst-episode, subchronic and chronic phases), ASD (175 patients), and ADHD (279 patients). Sex-speci /uniFB01 c WM tract normative development was modeled from diffusion spectrum imaging of 626 typically developing controls (5 -40 years). There were three signi /uniFB01 cant /uniFB01 ndings. First, the patterns of deviation and idiosyncrasy of WM tracts were similar between schizophrenia and ADHD alongside ASD, particularly at the earlier stages of schizophrenia relative to chronic stages. Second, using the WM deviation patterns as features, schizophrenia cannot be separated from neurodevelopmental disorders in the unsupervised machine learning algorithm. Lastly, the canonical correlation analysis showed schizophrenia, ADHD, and ASD shared linked cognitive dimensions driven by WM deviations. Together, our results provide new insights into the neurodevelopmental facet of schizophrenia and its brain basis. Individual ' s WM deviations may contribute to diverse arrays of cognitive function along a continuum with phenotypic expressions from typical neurodevelopmental disorders to schizophrenia.
Chien2022.pdf.md:19:Neurobiology underlying schizophrenia is elusive, limiting the development of effective treatment [1]. Among many hypotheses posited, emerging evidence from genetic, transcriptomic, epigenetic, and epidemiological data mainly converges on the neurodevelopmental origins of schizophrenia [2]. This neurodevelopmental model postulates that schizophrenic genetic risk compositions, alongside exogenous environmental input, subtly perturb early brain development. With these developmental insults, psychotic symptoms could develop later in late adolescence/early adulthood when maturational events impose increasing functional demands, leading to decompensation of the neural systems [2, 3]. This model may account for clinical heterogeneity given inter-individual differences in developmental risk factors [2]. Nonetheless, scarce neuroimaging studies have substantiated this heuristic speculation [2 -4].
Chien2022.pdf.md:21:The neurodevelopmental model considers schizophrenia as on a neurodevelopmental continuum [2], supported by the multifaceted relationships of schizophrenia with more typical, early-onset neurodevelopmental disorders, such as autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and attention-de /uniFB01 cit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD). Speci /uniFB01 -cally, both schizophrenia and ASD feature similar impairments in social cognition [5]. A subset of individuals with schizophrenia shows a prior history of ADHD before the onset of psychotic symptoms [6]. ADHD or ASD is diagnosed in many children at risk for schizophrenia [7]. Schizophrenia, ASD, and ADHD also share
Chien2022.pdf.md:29:Nonetheless, some contrasting reports against the close links between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders exist. For example, a recent study testing structural models of psychopathology at the genomic level suggests ASD and ADHD in the neurodevelopmental factor while schizophrenia in the thoughtproblems factor [13]. A secondary analysis from the ENIGMA consortium [14] has reported that the brain morphology is highly correlated between schizophrenia, major depressive, bipolar, and obsessive-compulsive disorders. Still, those in ASD and ADHD are independent of the common factor aggregating morphological alterations among these disorders. The inconsistency may be explained by the continuing brain and symptom changes at different stages of schizophrenia [1, 3, 4]. But these earlier studies did not consider the illness progression. Moreover, particularly for neuroimaging studies, brain maps of the ' average patient ' inferred from casecontrol differences might not be capable of capturing any particular individual, further resulting in heterogeneity across studies [15]. Therefore, normative modeling [15], combined with strati /uniFB01 cation based on disease stages of schizophrenia, is needed to better model individual variation in brain alterations and explain the relationships between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders.
Chien2022.pdf.md:31:Based on recent endeavors in schizophrenia [16] and neurodevelopmental disorders [17], the current study leveraged normative modeling to further investigate similarity and distinctness in deviations (to the age/sex norm) of white matter (WM) property, alongside the extent of inter-individual variations ( ' idiosyncrasy ' ), among different stages of schizophrenia, ASD, and ADHD. WM was explicitly targeted because it is the brain structural backbone essential in restraining functional network organization [18] and gray matter coupling [19], given schizophrenia, ASD, and ADHD as disorders of brain dysconnectivity [1, 8, 9]. Patients with schizophrenia show stable patterns of diffuse de /uniFB01 cits in WM connections [20]. The speci /uniFB01 c dysconnectivity may precede symptoms and is associated with cognitive de /uniFB01 cits [21 -30]. Similarly, cumulative evidence supports that individuals with ADHD exhibit distributed patterns of reduced WM connectivity along association and projection pathways, as well as altered microstructural integrity of frontal, striatal, and cerebellar circuits that are critical to behavioral control and motor function [31, 32]. Several altered tracts, such as superior and inferior longitudinal fasciculi, cingulum, and corpus callosum, reported in schizophrenia and ADHD are also impaired in ASD [33 -35]. Moreover, given the knowledge of WM lifespan trajectories, the investigation of WM is proposed as the frontline lore, particularly to reconcile the con /uniFB02 icting evidence in heuristic neurodevelopmental and other (e.g., neurodegenerative) models of schizophrenia [4]. Moving beyond the categorical characterization, the linked dimensions of cognition and brain metrics per the Research Domain Criteria (RDoC) framework [36], cutting across diagnostic boundaries may substantiate the neurodevelopmental facet of schizophrenia. Nonetheless, there has been no report using WM metrics on either categorical collations or dimensional brainbehavior relationships across schizophrenia, ADHD, and ASD.
Chien2022.pdf.md:33:Herein, we developed the normative model of WM microstructural property (generalized fractional anisotropy, GFA), as a function of age, across the lifespan using large-sampled single-site harmonized cross-sectional MRI data from more than 600 typically developing individuals (5 -40 yrs) [17]. This model established the normative ranges of variations in GFA, represented as functions of age and sex. Individual deviations from the norm, alongside variations in individual deviations, were then determined for around 300 individuals with schizophrenia, 300 with ADHD, and 200 with ASD. Further, we delineated linked dimensions of cognition and WM tract /uniFB01 ngerprints, as quanti /uniFB01 ed by the degree of deviation in each tract [36], re /uniFB02 ecting the potential shared neural correlates underlying cognitive impairments across schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders [1, 8, 9]. We hypothesized that the WM pro /uniFB01 les of deviance and idiosyncrasy in schizophrenia, especially in the earlier phase of the illness, would be similar to ASD and ADHD. However, as schizophrenia progressed from the /uniFB01 rst episode to the chronic phase (based on the strati /uniFB01 cation), the level of this similarity among schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders would decrease. We also hypothesized that there would exist different linked dimensions of cognitive function and WM deviation pro /uniFB01 les, cutting cross schizophrenia, and neurodevelopmental disorders.
Chien2022.pdf.md:43:## Image acquisition and analysis
Chien2022.pdf.md:45:Participants received MRI scans on the same 3-Tesla Siemens TIM Trio scanner with a 32-channel phased-array head coil. The diffusion spectrum imaging (DSI) protocol was detailed elsewhere [17], with 102 diffusionencoding directions with a maximal b value of 4000 s/mm 2 , using a singleshot spin-echo echo-planar imaging sequence with twice-refocused diffusion-sensitive gradients to reduce distortion caused by eddy currents [50]. Before analysis, the rigorous quality control of MRI data was implemented by excluding those with low signal-noise ratio (SNR) or high signal-dropout counts (SDC), a proxy of in-scanner motion levels. As a result, SNR and SDC were comparable between these groups. The diffusion pro /uniFB01 les of each person included GFA of 45 major WM tracts ([17]; STable 4), prede /uniFB01 ned on an open-sourced DSI template [51] through deterministic streamline-based tractography [52]. GFA, providing a broad measure of WM axonal health in the DSI framework, was computed via mean-apparent propagator models [53] and sampled in the native space along each WM tract. The sampling coordinates in the native space were transformed from the template to individual MRI datasets with the corresponding deformation
Chien2022.pdf.md:49:Table 1. Demographic features and image data quality of all participants.
Chien2022.pdf.md:74:maps through diffeomorphic registration [51]. MRI acquisition, quality control, preprocessing, and tractography are detailed elsewhere [17].
Chien2022.pdf.md:76:## Statistical analysis, standardized normative models
Chien2022.pdf.md:78:Individualized tract-deviation pro /uniFB01 les comprised GFA Z -scores of each WM tract derived from the sex-speci /uniFB01 c normative age models, constructed from 626 TDCs (Male/Female: 376/250; age range 5 -40, 20.16 ± 8.5 yrs) [17]. A moving average approach was used to obtain the unbiased mean and variance estimates for the GFA metric in each age range. Next, GFA was transformed into Z -scores, yielding individualized tract-alteration pro /uniFB01 les. Each group ' s distributions of GFA Z -scores of WM tracts are presented in STable 5. The derived Z -scores of clinical groups and TDC were not correlated with age, SNR, or in-scanner motion levels (SFig. 1).
Chien2022.pdf.md:80:Within the schizophrenia subgroups, the effect sizes of one-sample t -tests on GFA Z -scores of each WM tract for each subgroup re /uniFB02 ect the deviation extent (relative to the TDC norm) of this speci /uniFB01 c tract in this particular group. When the FDR-corrected alpha of a one-sample test on a speci /uniFB01 c tract of a schizophrenia subgroup is &lt;0.05, it denotes that this tract signi /uniFB01 cantly deviates from the norm in this subgroup (SFig. 2). We used the F -ratio (a tract GFA Z -score variance of a subgroup divided by this tract variance of the TDC norm) of the Ftest (of equality of variances) [54] to estimate idiosyncrasy pro /uniFB01 les of WM tracts for each subgroup [17]. The idiosyncrasy of a tract of a subgroup indicates the variance of this tract ' s GFA Z -score distribution in this subgroup relative to that in the norm (SFig. 3).
Chien2022.pdf.md:82:To investigate the categorical relationships between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders, between-group similarity and difference in the extents of GFA deviation, alongside idiosyncrasy, were evaluated. Strati /uniFB01 ed analyses of /uniFB01 rst-episode schizophrenia (FES), subchronic, and chronic schizophrenia were implemented to specify whether people in the early course of schizophrenia present WM alterations more similar to neurodevelopmental disorders than patients with later-stage schizophrenia. Betweengroup differences (e.g., schizophrenia vs. ASD, schizophrenia vs. ADHD, etc.) of deviation and idiosyncrasy based on GFA Z -scores were evaluated with a twosample t -test and F -test (schizophrenia ' s WM variances divided by neurodevelopmental ' s WM variances), respectively. Although the normative modeling has taken the age and sex effects into account when yielding WM deviation pro /uniFB01 les, we performed the propensity score weights matching for age [55] between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders as the main results. We repeated this categorical analysis based on the entire sample of neurodevelopmental disorders to complement the main /uniFB01 ndings.
Chien2022.pdf.md:84:Spearman ' s correlation ( ρ ) of the WM tract deviation and idiosyncrasy pro /uniFB01 les were calculated to explore between-group similarity further. High correlations of WM tract pro /uniFB01 les between groups indicate similarity regardless of absolute values. The false-discovery rate was employed to correct for multiple comparisons of 45 WM tracts for each clinical group of interest, separately for deviation, idiosyncrasy, and correlation metrics. This between-diagnosis correlation was also carried out separately between each stage of schizophrenia and their age-matched counterparts of ASD or ADHD. The details regarding data distributions and the rationale for the statistical test selection are described elsewhere [17].
Chien2022.pdf.md:88:We conducted unsupervised machine learning based on the individual ' s brain features to investigate whether schizophrenia, ASD, or ADHD (using the entire sample) could be clustered into distinct groups that correspond to clinical diagnoses or hidden features. Both Ward ' s hierarchical clustering and k-means clustering were applied to the /uniFB01 rst 12 components extracted from the GFA Z -score pro /uniFB01 les of each individual using the principal component analysis Fig. 3A). These 12 components explained at least 1% of the variation separately; about 86% of the variance was explained by these components together. The optimal number of clustering was de /uniFB01 ned by Gap statistics [56], whereby the largest jump in within-cluster distance relative to a null reference distribution occurred.
Chien2022.pdf.md:94:covariation between GFA Z-scores and cognitive measures [57]. Each mode consists of canonical variables of GFA-Z and cognitive measures that maximally correlate. Each original variable ' s relative contributions in each mode are re /uniFB02 ected by the canonical loadings (Fig. 4), the linear correlations ( r ) between the canonical variates, and the original variables in this set [17]. Weused100,000 permutations of the rows of one matrix relative to the other to estimate statistical signi /uniFB01 cance at FWE-corrected alpha &lt;0.05.
Chien2022.pdf.md:98:As shown in SFig. 2, participants with schizophrenia showed widespread signi /uniFB01 cantly negative deviations relative to the age and sex norm in most major WM tracts. Patients with chronic schizophrenia showed more pronounced whole-brain WM deviations than patients with earlier-stage schizophrenia in terms of effect sizes of deviation (maximum Cohen ' s d : -0.93 in chronic vs. -0.72 in FES or -0.79 in subchronic) and the amounts of altered tracts (Cohen ' s d &lt; -0.5: 18 tracts in the chronic stage, 11 tracts in FES and 6 tracts in subchronic; chi-square test [FES vs. chronic] p = 0.11, [subchronic vs. chronic] p = 0.004). Regarding the idiosyncrasy metric (SFig. 3), patients with FES showed less pronounced interindividual variability in GFA deviation to the norm relative to those in patients with subchronic and chronic schizophrenia (maximum Fratio: 3.22 in chronic, 2.58 in subchronic, 2.29 in FES; 18 tracts in FES, 3 in subchronic, and 12 tracts in chronic schizophrenia were not signi /uniFB01 cantly idiosyncratic, respectively).
