3:**Neurodevelopmental model of schizophrenia revisited: similarity in individual deviation and idiosyncrasy from the normative model of whole-brain white matter tracts and shared brain-cognition covariation with ADHD and ASD**
83:As shown in SFigure 4-5 (FDR-corrected significant results of t-test and F-test, respectively), the GFA of WM tracts of schizophrenia has more negative deviation and was more idiosyncratic than ASD or ADHD, with a remarkable trend that the longer duration of illness in schizophrenia was, the greater differences in deviation/idiosyncrasy relative to neurodevelopmental disorders were. That is, the absolute values of Cohen's d and F-ratio were larger in chronic schizophrenia vs. neurodevelopmental disorders pairs than those in the earlier stages (FES and subchronic schizophrenia) vs. neurodevelopmental pairs. Specifically, compared to patients with schizophrenia across the disease stages, people with ADHD, relative to people with ASD, showed greater extents (i.e., more tracts involved and larger effect sizes) of differences in deviance and idiosyncrasy. Among the neural tracts, the bilateral fornix (-1.04≤Cohen's d≤-0.44), frontostriatal tracts (-0.92≤Cohen's d≤-0.31), inferior fronto-occipital fasciculi (IFOF, -0.82≤Cohen's d≤-0.33), thalamic radiations (-0.64≤Cohen's d≤-0.33), corpus callosum-temporal lobe (-0.70≤Cohen's d≤-0.28), right perpendicular fasciculus (-0.84≤Cohen's d≤-0.31), and right superior longitudinal fasciculus-I (-0.72≤Cohen's d≤-0.28) showed prominent deviance patterns in schizophrenia, especially chronic schizophrenia (all Cohen's d≤-0.58 among these tracts), compared to neurodevelopmental disorders (SFigure 4A and SFigure 5A). Moreover, the thalamic radiations (1.48≤F-ratio≤2.87), frontostriatal tracts (1.40≤F-ratio≤2.67), and cingulum-hippocampal (1.61≤F-ratio≤2.66) showed the greatest differences in idiosyncrasy in schizophrenia (F-ratio $\geq$ 2.4) (SFigure 4B and SFigure 5B).
85:SFigure 7 and STable 8A show the overall similarity of deviance and idiosyncrasy of each tract between different stages of schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders. Significant correlations were noted between GFA Z-score mean deviation of FES, and ASD ( *ρ* =0.49, *p* =.001) and ADHD ( *ρ* =0.42, *p* =.004), respectively. A significant correlation between GFA Z-score idiosyncrasy of FES was noted with ASD ( *ρ* =0.41, *p* =.005), but not with ADHD (SFigure 7B). Significant correlations were noted between GFA Z-score mean deviation of subchronic schizophrenia and ASD ( *ρ* =0.54, *p* &lt;.001) and ADHD ( *ρ* =0.52, *p* &lt;.001), respectively. A significant correlation between GFA Z-score idiosyncrasy of subchronic schizophrenia was noted with ASD ( *ρ* =0.32, *p* =.034), but marginally significant with ADHD ( *p* =.05). No correlation was noted between either deviation or idiosyncrasy in chronic schizophrenia and ASD or ADHD, respectively (SFigure 7B). The deviance in ASD had significantly higher correlations with that in FES than with that in chronic schizophrenia (FES-ASD 𝜌=0.49 &gt; chronic-ASD 𝜌=0.12; z=1.90, *p* =.028) (STable 8B).
